WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.330
This is a packet and bottle
of freeze-dried urine,

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.620
and the United States government
will sell it to you for $983,

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.450
plus shipping.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.040
Which is perhaps the strangest sentence I've
ever started a video with,

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.040
but it’s true.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.910
What's even stranger is:
that’s a pretty reasonable price.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.560
These are two of more than 1,000
Standard Reference Materials

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.510
that are kept here at the National Institute
of Standards and Technology.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:26.720
Here at NIST, we produce
Standard Reference Materials

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.680
or SRMs for short.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.300
SRMs are super characterised materials that
our customers use

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.870
to make sure their measurements
on their materials and processes

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.780
are accurate and reliable.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.750
When a customer buys one of our SRMs,

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.530
they get both a unit of material
and a certificate of analysis.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.170
Each property, whether it’s a chemical constituent

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.489
or a physical characteristic of the material

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:50.830
is a certified property

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.059
and it's measured most accurately.

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:57.489
The certificate of analysis is just as important
as the material itself.

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:01.260
Without the certificate and all the information
that’s inside of it,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.820
the SRM is just another bottle of stuff.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.539
We have a variety of food materials in our
SRM catalogue,

00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:09.030
so this is slurried spinach.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.889
When you slurry the spinach, it's nice and
homogenous.

00:01:11.889 --> 00:01:15.899
You sample a little bit from the top, it’s
the same as sampling from the bottom.

00:01:15.899 --> 00:01:19.259
We also have our infamous domestic sludge,

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:21.939
from a waste water treatment process,

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:28.289
that has been dried, ground up, homogenised
into this nice, fine powder.

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:29.289
We take this material.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.579
We certify various toxic elements
within the sludge,

00:01:32.579 --> 00:01:34.700
that’s what it’s used for.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:40.630
We also have the microsphere slide that contains
the space beads.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:44.079
They're 10 micron-wide, polystyrene spheres

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:47.099
that were formed on
the space shuttle in zero gravity.

00:01:47.099 --> 00:01:52.100
We have to store some of our more
sensitive materials in cryocooled conditions.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.409
We have our peanut butter, right there in
a glass jar.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:59.209
Oh, it’s crackling.

00:01:59.209 --> 00:02:03.600
Then, we have our whale blubber,
organics in whale blubber.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.530
This is actually whale blubber tissue from
a beached whale.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:12.150
It's for tracking organic contaminants in
the environment,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.709
and whales are the end of the food chain.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:18.430
Every one of these reference materials has
to be exactly the same,

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.170
to an incredibly precise margin.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.100
They’ll be used by people around the world
to calibrate and test machinery.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.360
Now, other places make
reference materials too.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.780
It's just that everything in this warehouse
has the US Government stamp of approval on it.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:37.700
Now, just to be clear, the vast majority of
reference materials here are pretty dull.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.180
They're for industrial processes, so they're
metal, or glass, or pure elements.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.180
There are three types of bauxite: from Suriname,
Jamaica or the Dominican Republic.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.329
There's even really precise mixes of gases
available,

00:02:49.329 --> 00:02:51.090
along with some radioactive elements.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.799
Sometimes, it takes us years to actually procure
a new material

00:02:55.799 --> 00:02:58.200
that becomes an SRM a couple of years later.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.560
We buy industrial products directly.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.970
It comes from a different source each time
we make it,

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.459
but we make it the same way,

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:07.709
and we characterise it in the same fashion.

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:11.200
Either we determine the chemical composition
or its physical characteristics,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.150
but we do that with accuracy.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:18.069
In other cases, we have SRMs that are used
to determine how they break.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:21.219
We measure over and over again,
with different methods,

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:25.790
to ensure that what we're measuring is the
true value in that particular material.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.849
The material itself is not precise.

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:29.989
The measurements that characterise it
are precise.

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:31.810
So if you haven’t worked it out yet,

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:35.030
the reason that freeze-dried urine is one
of the reference materials

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:39.159
is because there are folks out there with
machines that test urine.

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.930
They're either testing for drugs,

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:44.129
so the Institute will sell you urine laced
with exact amounts

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:47.269
of the metabolites of cannabis or cocaine,

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:50.409
or they're testing for poisons and toxic elements.

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:52.769
So if you need to calibrate
one of those testing machines,

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:55.969
to make sure that the results are accurate,

00:03:55.969 --> 00:03:58.100
then the folks here
are the ones you're going to call.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.389
Thank you to everyone here at the National
Institute of Standards and Technology.

00:04:01.389 --> 00:04:03.840
Pull down the description for more about Standard
Reference Materials

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.020
and about their work.

